From 7576e863090ec8ec1ed991b091939ab93684eff3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: George Watson <george@hellas.ai>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 00:31:17 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] usb: dwc2: gadget: ask u_ether for DMA-friendly RX buffers

In DMA mode the core needs 32-bit aligned buffers, and any request whose
buffer is not aligned is bounced: dwc2_hsotg_handle_unaligned_buf_start()
kmalloc()s a copy with GFP_ATOMIC, the DMA lands there, and the data is
copied into the real buffer on completion. u_ether reserves NET_IP_ALIGN
(2) bytes at the head of every RX skb so the IP header ends up aligned,
which makes every single RX request take that bounce path: an atomic
allocation, a cache invalidate of the bounce buffer and a full-frame
memcpy per received packet. On a 1 GHz single-core C906 (SG2002) that
is a visible slice of the softirq time that saturates the CPU at
high-speed.

The DDMA OUT path also rounds the programmed transfer length up to a
multiple of the endpoint's maximum packet size without checking that the
request buffer is that large; u_ether only sizes RX buffers that way
when the UDC asks for it.

Set quirk_avoids_skb_reserve and quirk_ep_out_aligned_size whenever the
gadget runs with DMA, as renesas_usbf, cdns3 and dwc3 do for the same
reasons. The quirks are consumed only by u_ether, so other functions are
unaffected.

Signed-off-by: George Watson <george@hellas.ai>
---
 drivers/usb/dwc2/gadget.c | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/dwc2/gadget.c b/drivers/usb/dwc2/gadget.c
index 102169b..c5e960a 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/dwc2/gadget.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc2/gadget.c
@@ -5021,6 +5021,22 @@ int dwc2_gadget_init(struct dwc2_hsotg *hsotg)
 	hsotg->gadget.otg_caps = &hsotg->params.otg_caps;
 	hsotg->remote_wakeup_allowed = 0;
 
+	/*
+	 * In DMA mode the core needs 32-bit aligned buffers and OUT transfer
+	 * sizes that are a multiple of the endpoint's maximum packet size.
+	 * Unaligned requests fall back to a kmalloc()ed bounce buffer plus a
+	 * copy on every transfer (dwc2_hsotg_handle_unaligned_buf_start()),
+	 * and the DDMA OUT path silently rounds the programmed length up to a
+	 * packet boundary. The Ethernet gadgets reserve NET_IP_ALIGN bytes in
+	 * every RX skb, which makes every RX request unaligned. Ask u_ether
+	 * for aligned, packet-sized buffers instead; an unaligned IP header
+	 * costs far less than bouncing every frame.
+	 */
+	if (using_dma(hsotg)) {
+		hsotg->gadget.quirk_avoids_skb_reserve = 1;
+		hsotg->gadget.quirk_ep_out_aligned_size = 1;
+	}
+
 	if (hsotg->params.lpm)
 		hsotg->gadget.lpm_capable = true;
 
-- 
